[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332270"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332292/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e21e6871-9319-5a36-9bc9-3d9b60ab02ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332292/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "b80ac86f-4ee0-5a4b-be80-3ed623d8c1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332292/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a05bd5a-2fc8-5178-bca4-d955ae19c687" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332292/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-332292/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-332292/field-1/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332292" :question/id #uuid "5ac3284f-adb8-5c84-8842-ce33dc9e9c4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332292" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha =-10\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332292/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332292"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332307/field-1/a84b91c5483d65945964f22df3098373c5ac598a7cb32934669ca305d66926b9" :answer/id #uuid "729f2eee-1524-5df7-86de-567f97f434c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332307/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b11e040d-97c7-5771-b1d3-2a5f4f468246" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332307/field-1/a84b91c5483d65945964f22df3098373c5ac598a7cb32934669ca305d66926b9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332307/field-1/a84b91c5483d65945964f22df3098373c5ac598a7cb32934669ca305d66926b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332307" :question/id #uuid "bf6e3127-f9d0-53df-9d6d-0255fcb68d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332307" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332307/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332307"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332539/field-1/530721d01ebecc68569f558ebc6b23a8ed7dd2d7cf9b7a8abe6885816fb8abc9" :answer/id #uuid "565f4ebb-393e-543c-a2e5-4d7204aa5746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-5x}+x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332539/field-1/00a96998a6207e075a4d8294eeeff8c7b24bca065e8db38d5f264e380bbbadfa" :answer/id #uuid "a41db1db-77de-5d53-a5a9-4f3699888aa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-5x}+x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332539/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "55924886-94d9-5faa-aed2-a5e64cb8dd9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332539/field-1/530721d01ebecc68569f558ebc6b23a8ed7dd2d7cf9b7a8abe6885816fb8abc9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332539/field-1/530721d01ebecc68569f558ebc6b23a8ed7dd2d7cf9b7a8abe6885816fb8abc9" "q-332539/field-1/00a96998a6207e075a4d8294eeeff8c7b24bca065e8db38d5f264e380bbbadfa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332539" :question/id #uuid "2f3493ad-3f34-5e86-8757-be73d8ebaba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332539" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=5x-4.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\alpha x+(\\alpha +5\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +5\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+x-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332539/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332539"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection/332ef53ce7e182153b84582379d804e0db09a75f765ec10d5e4b84632634fa94" :answer/id #uuid "65f2dec7-4844-5eb8-9dcb-d1353697d337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection/f7d783c527f8133b169ff2a659e7bd9a6cb09517cf980c9d2fdf8ee4c5cf1632" :answer/id #uuid "e654ca1e-d9b9-552e-92dd-467b83196767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection/e6d938cc45985b6c540c2f5fe1e2c904186e0ca87158c1b0baa7a2403515fda5" :answer/id #uuid "4c7b5bd1-2b89-5b02-a941-401bce7ac108" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection/8dd825eacdde93307261605129ccb5ffdab4872ebacabe823deeafe5083b82fb" :answer/id #uuid "53821809-2f73-5757-b05d-6d009ddf63a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection/803092dea89147cc70cd5750de9d079262c377082dac78cbcf6a880840d7cda5" :answer/id #uuid "7013dc26-2524-59d0-b276-ed2b45dcffc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0a4f29-2bef-5a56-913a-703b8badacaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333042/selection/8dd825eacdde93307261605129ccb5ffdab4872ebacabe823deeafe5083b82fb" :answer-field/choices ["q-333042/selection/332ef53ce7e182153b84582379d804e0db09a75f765ec10d5e4b84632634fa94" "q-333042/selection/f7d783c527f8133b169ff2a659e7bd9a6cb09517cf980c9d2fdf8ee4c5cf1632" "q-333042/selection/e6d938cc45985b6c540c2f5fe1e2c904186e0ca87158c1b0baa7a2403515fda5" "q-333042/selection/8dd825eacdde93307261605129ccb5ffdab4872ebacabe823deeafe5083b82fb" "q-333042/selection/803092dea89147cc70cd5750de9d079262c377082dac78cbcf6a880840d7cda5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333042" :question/id #uuid "53a56fd0-2d25-5ed0-9417-d9b97a813796" :question/math-academy-id "q-333042" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -4\\beta )x+(\\beta -4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -4\\beta =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -4\\gamma =-3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-333042/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333042"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
